# Line-ending policy.
#
# Every tracked text file is already LF in the index (verified: 1157 i/lf, 0 i/crlf),
# but nothing declared it, so normalization depended on each developer's global
# core.autocrlf. On Windows that means files check out as CRLF while the generators
# and the snapshot writer emit LF -- so `npm test` and `npm run docs:deps` leave the
# tree dirty with changes that contain no content difference at all. A dirty tree
# that means nothing is worse than noise: it hides a dirty tree that means something.
#
# `text=auto` fixes the index side for everyone regardless of their global config.
* text=auto

# These files are WRITTEN BY TOOLS that always emit LF. Checking them out as CRLF
# guarantees a spurious diff the moment the tool runs, so they are LF in the working
# tree too.
*.snap text eol=lf
docs/architecture/** text eol=lf
# The git hooks run under sh, which fails on a CR ("unset NAME\r" is an invalid name under set -e).
# With core.autocrlf=true a Windows checkout would write them CRLF, so they are LF everywhere.
.githooks/** text eol=lf
# Also tool-written, and so LF in the working tree: the exported data (catalog:json, atlas:json,
# atlas:witness-results), the study results (atlas:study) and the benchmark fixtures (the
# benchmark scripts). A fresh-clone reproduction on 2026-09-23 found all eight regenerated
# artifacts byte-identical to their committed blobs, yet shown as modified, for this reason.
data/** text eol=lf
docs/research/atlas-study-results.md text eol=lf
tests/fixtures/atlas/benchmark/** text eol=lf
# Written by `bun run atlas:formal-gate -- --write-captured`, which emits LF.
formal/physlib/captured/** text eol=lf
# Written by `bun run atlas:c3-export` and `bun run atlas:quote-check -- --write`, which emit LF. The
# criterion 3 files are frozen by the SHA-256 of their LF bytes, so a CRLF checkout would make a plain
# sha256sum disagree with the pre-registered hash.
docs/research/criterion3/** text eol=lf
# The labeler files arrive with CRLF line ends, and their pre-registered SHA-256 is of those exact bytes.
# Git must store them byte for byte, so no line-end conversion applies (this later rule wins).
docs/research/criterion3/labels/** -text
docs/research/phase-1-citation-quote-check.out.md text eol=lf
# Written by tests/fixtures/probe-study/generate.mjs, which emits LF; the probe-study tests compare the
# committed files with the generator output and edit the CSV text line by line. A Windows checkout with
# core.autocrlf=true wrote them CRLF and four tests failed on a tree with no content difference.
tests/fixtures/probe-study/** text eol=lf

# Binary-ish: never touch these.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
